1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,520
A forest in Horizon Zero Dawn

2
00:00:01,520 --> 00:00:04,380
takes more than simply adding trees.

3
00:00:04,380 --> 00:00:05,460
Paths need to remain clear,

4
00:00:05,460 --> 00:00:07,960
while suitable plants fill each region.

5
00:00:07,960 --> 00:00:10,640
The developers turn artist-defined data and rules

6
00:00:10,640 --> 00:00:13,720
into placements on the GPU, the graphics processor.

7
00:00:13,720 --> 00:00:15,540
The starting point is a map.

8
00:00:15,540 --> 00:00:17,000
It stores where trees should be dense

9
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:20,080
and where water and paths are located.

10
00:00:20,080 --> 00:00:22,840
Plant types and appearance are set by region.

11
00:00:22,840 --> 00:00:25,960
Artists adjust the maps and plant assets.

12
00:00:25,960 --> 00:00:28,640
Automated placement still has human direction.

13
00:00:28,640 --> 00:00:31,000
Next, build a density map.

14
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:33,660
It determines how much vegetation to place.

15
00:00:33,660 --> 00:00:35,980
Exclude areas occupied by roads and water.

16
00:00:35,980 --> 00:00:39,040
Combine the conditions into a map for placement.

17
00:00:39,040 --> 00:00:41,160
Now compare candidate points with the map.

18
00:00:41,160 --> 00:00:42,960
Check the density condition at each candidate

19
00:00:42,960 --> 00:00:44,700
and retain the points that pass.

20
00:00:44,700 --> 00:00:47,880
Spacing and point patterns are also controlled.

21
00:00:47,880 --> 00:00:50,960
This differs from piling trees at arbitrary positions.

22
00:00:50,960 --> 00:00:53,500
Actual objects are placed at the remaining points.

23
00:00:53,500 --> 00:00:56,040
Align them with the terrain’s height and orientation,

24
00:00:56,040 --> 00:00:58,800
and add variation in scale or rotation.

25
00:00:58,800 --> 00:00:59,640
A point in the map becomes

26
00:00:59,640 --> 00:01:02,440
a tuft of grass or a tree on screen.

27
00:01:02,440 --> 00:01:05,220
More placement layers can introduce overlaps.

28
00:01:05,220 --> 00:01:06,740
The presentation explains how multiple layers

29
00:01:06,740 --> 00:01:10,320
can be coordinated by sharing density ranges.

30
00:01:10,320 --> 00:01:14,560
Filling grass, rocks and trees separately is not enough.

31
00:01:14,560 --> 00:01:17,620
In order: artist maps, placement conditions,

32
00:01:17,620 --> 00:01:20,480
candidate tests, then actual objects.

33
00:01:20,480 --> 00:01:22,120
With the computer handling repetitive placement,

34
00:01:22,120 --> 00:01:24,580
artists can refine the forest’s character

35
00:01:24,580 --> 00:01:26,040
and the flow of its paths more quickly.
